/*
 * The main page of the API reference and the page on the SDF format, which the
 * descriptions of the single functions refer to.
 */

/**
 * @mainpage libsdfio
 *
 * libsdfio is a portable C library without dependencies that reads and writes
 * the SDF surface data files of ISO 25178-71. An SDF file holds the heights of
 * a profile or of an areal surface measurement on a rectangular grid. It is
 * used as a software measurement standard, a set of reference data with which
 * the software of surface texture measuring instruments is verified, and as an
 * interchange format for topography data.
 *
 * @section features Features
 * - Implemented according to ISO 25178-71:2026, with the revisions ISO-1.0 and
 *   ISO-2.0.
 * - Access to the data as heights in metres or as the coded values of the
 *   file.
 * - Application-side buffers with arbitrary strides.
 * - Custom allocators.
 * - Files, blocks of memory and streams, including pipes, as sources and sinks.
 * - Thread support: the library holds no global state, so threads use handles
 *   of their own at the same time.
 *
 * @section limitations Limitations
 * - A handle must not be shared between threads.
 * - The locale must not be changed while a call is in progress.
 * - Rows can be read only once and in the order of the file.
 * - A failure in the middle of the data area makes the handle refuse further
 *   reads and writes.
 * - The library and the program that calls it have to be built with the same
 *   width of `time_t`, see @ref building_build.
 * - Files of the format BCR-1.0, the predecessor of ISO-1.0, are not supported.
 *
 * @section building_build Building and Installing
 * @code{.sh}
 * cmake -S . -B build
 * cmake --build build
 * ctest --test-dir build
 * cmake --install build --prefix /path/to/prefix
 * @endcode
 *
 * With Visual Studio, configure with `-A x64` (or `Win32`, `ARM64`) and name
 * the configuration in the other commands: `--config Release`, and
 * `-C Release` for `ctest`.
 *
 * These CMake options control which components are built:
 *
 * <table class="doxtable">
 * <tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Builds</th></tr>
 * <tr><td>SDFIO_BUILD_EXAMPLES</td><td>OFF</td><td>The examples.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>SDFIO_BUILD_EXTERNAL_EXAMPLES</td><td>OFF</td><td>The examples that
 * use external libraries.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>SDFIO_BUILD_DOCS</td><td>OFF</td><td>This API reference, with
 * Doxygen, as the target `docs`.</td></tr>
 * </table>
 *
 * The dates of the interface are `time_t` values, which the library reads as
 * integer seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, as POSIX defines them. The
 * library and the program that calls it have to be built with the same width
 * of `time_t`, and the build of the library does not select it. Where `time_t`
 * is already 64 bits, as on 64-bit targets and with the UCRT, nothing is
 * needed. A 32-bit host selects a 64-bit `time_t` with `-D_TIME_BITS=64` and
 * `-D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64` on glibc, which rejects `_TIME_BITS=64` without the
 * second flag, and with `-D_TIME_BITS=64` on MinGW-w64 with the msvcrt runtime.
 * The flags go to the compiler of the program and to the compiler of the
 * library, for example when the library is built from source:
 * @code{.sh}
 * cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-D_TIME_BITS=64 -D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64"
 * @endcode
 * A date that a 32-bit `time_t` cannot hold reads as SDFIO_NO_TIME.
 *
 * @section building_use Using the Library
 * An installed library provides the CMake target `sdfio::sdfio`:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * find_package(sdfio CONFIG REQUIRED)
 * add_executable(main main.c)
 * target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE sdfio::sdfio)
 * @endcode
 *
 * The program includes `<sdfio/sdfio.h>`, and `<sdfio/file.h>` for the
 * functions that open files. The programs under Examples show typical uses.
 * Without CMake, pkg-config gives the flags:
 * @code{.sh}
 * cc main.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs sdfio) -o main
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section building_checks Running the Checks
 * The continuous integration runs its jobs with `.github/ci/run.sh`, which
 * runs them on a developer machine in the same way. The first command lists
 * the jobs and the second runs one of them:
 * @code{.sh}
 * .github/ci/run.sh --list
 * .github/ci/run.sh gcc-x86-64-linux
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @page format SDF Format
 * @details An SDF file holds the heights of a profile or of an areal surface
 * measurement on a rectangular grid. It is used as a software measurement
 * standard, a set of reference data with which the software of surface texture
 * measuring instruments is verified, and as an interchange format for
 * topography data. A file consists of three records: the header, the data area
 * and the trailer. The file name extension is `.sdf`. A file has a 7-bit ASCII
 * or a binary format. Both hold the same information. A reader or a writer
 * supports a format after it is added with sdfio_reader_add_binary(),
 * sdfio_reader_add_ascii() or one of the two functions of the writer. The file
 * starts with a magic of 8 characters, which the standard calls the version
 * number. Its first character is `a` for the ASCII and `b` for the binary
 * format, and the rest names the version: `aISO-1.0`, `aISO-2.0`, `bISO-1.0`
 * and `bISO-2.0`. The library calls the version a revision, ISO-1.0 and
 * ISO-2.0. ISO-1.0 is the standardized form of BCR-1.0, the format that
 * preceded the standard, described by K. J. Stout et al., The Development of
 * Methods for the Characterisation of Roughness in Three Dimensions, EUR 15178
 * EN, 1993. ISO-1.0 allows the data types int16_t, int32_t and double for the
 * data area, stores the numbers of columns and rows of a binary file as
 * uint16_t and has dates without a time zone. ISO-2.0 adds int8_t and float for
 * the data area, stores the numbers as uint32_t, has dates in UTC and a trailer
 * in the form of the header.
 *
 * @section format_types Data Types
 * The values of the data area have one of these data types, which the header
 * selects with its code.
 *
 * <table class="doxtable">
 * <tr><th>Data
 *   Type</th><th>Code</th><th>Size</th><th>ISO-1.0</th><th>ISO-2.0</th><th>Value
 *   of a Point That Is Not Measured</th></tr>
 * <tr><td>float (binary32)</td><td>3</td><td>4
 *   bytes</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>-FLT_MAX (SDFIO_BAD_FLOAT)</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>int8_t</td><td>4</td><td>1
 *   byte</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>INT8_MIN (SDFIO_BAD_INT8)</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>int16_t</td><td>5</td><td>2
 *   bytes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>INT16_MIN (SDFIO_BAD_INT16)</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>int32_t</td><td>6</td><td>4
 *   bytes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>INT32_MIN (SDFIO_BAD_INT32)</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>double (binary64)</td><td>7</td><td>8
 *   bytes</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>-DBL_MAX (SDFIO_BAD_DOUBLE)</td></tr>
 * </table>
 *
 * A point that is not measured, or is spurious, is labelled with the minimum
 * value of the data type, which the standard does not permit for any valid
 * coded value. An ASCII file writes the string `BAD` instead.
 *
 * @section format_header Header
 * <table class="doxtable">
 * <tr><th>Field Name</th><th>Binary Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>ManufacID</code></td><td>char[10]</td><td>Manufacturer-specific
 *   identifier.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>CreateDate</code></td><td>char[12]</td><td>When the data was
 *   generated.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>ModDate</code></td><td>char[12]</td><td>When the file was last
 *   modified.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>NumPoints</code></td><td>uint16_t or uint32_t</td><td>Number
 *   of columns M, the points along x.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>NumProfiles</code></td><td>uint16_t or uint32_t</td><td>Number
 *   of rows N, the points along y. 1 for a profile.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>Xscale</code></td><td>double</td><td>Sampling interval
 *   along x in metres.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>Yscale</code></td><td>double</td><td>Sampling interval
 *   along y in metres, meaningless for a profile.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>Zscale</code></td><td>double</td><td>Positive factor that
 *   turns a coded value into metres.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>Zresolution</code></td><td>double</td><td>Quantization
 *   step of the instrument in metres, or a negative number if it is
 *   unknown.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>Compression</code></td><td>uint8_t</td><td>0, no
 *   compression.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>DataType</code></td><td>uint8_t</td><td>Code of the data
 *   type of the values in the data area, see Data Types.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td><code>CheckType</code></td><td>uint8_t</td><td>0, no
 *   checksum.</td></tr>
 * </table>
 *
 * The names `NumPoints` and `NumProfiles` stem from tactile instruments that
 * measure profiles: `NumPoints` is the number of points of a profile and
 * `NumProfiles` the number of profiles. In a binary file the fields follow the
 * magic in the order of the table. In an ASCII file the magic is the first
 * line, and the other fields can appear in any order.
 *
 * A date is a string of 12 digits, `DDMMYYYYhhmm`: day, month, year, hour and
 * minute, with leading zeros. The date of an ISO-2.0 file is UTC. The dates of
 * an ISO-1.0 file are the local clock reading of the writer, and the file does
 * not record the time zone.
 *
 * The library handles every date as a UTC time: the functions of the header
 * take and return a `time_t` in UTC. A date of an ISO-2.0 file needs no
 * conversion. A date of an ISO-1.0 file is converted by a time zone, which
 * turns the clock reading into UTC when a reader opens the file, and the UTC
 * time back into a clock reading when a writer writes the header. The program
 * sets the time zone with sdfio_reader_set_time_zone() or
 * sdfio_writer_set_time_zone(). sdfio_system_time_zone() is the time zone of
 * the machine that runs the program, and a program passes its own conversion
 * as an sdfio_time_zone. Without a time zone the library takes the clock
 * reading as UTC. The UTC time is correct only if the time zone is the one in
 * which the file was written.
 *
 * @section format_data Data Area
 * The data area holds the matrix W of the coded values with N rows and M
 * columns, row by row in the order of the position along y. The coded value
 * `w(i,j)` of the row i and the column j, counted from 1, belongs to the point
 *
 * @code
 * x = (j - 1) * Xscale     y = (i - 1) * Yscale     z = w(i,j) * Zscale
 * @endcode
 *
 * in a right-handed coordinate system: x grows with the column, y with the row,
 * and z points to the viewer who looks from above. The first row has y = 0.
 * Seen from above with x to the right, y grows upward, so the first row of the
 * file is the bottom row of the picture. sdfio_read_data() and
 * sdfio_read_heights() deliver the rows in this order, and the writing
 * functions take them in this order. Image libraries count the rows from the
 * top, so a program that shows a surface unchanged shows it upside down.
 *
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * seen from above:
 *
 *    y (along the rows)
 *    ^
 *    |
 *    |
 *    (o)------> x (along the columns)
 *
 *    z comes out of the page, to the viewer
 * @endcode
 *
 * A coded value is not a length. It becomes the height of the point in metres
 * when it is multiplied by the z-scale. A file of an integer type spends the
 * range of the type on the heights of the measurement, and the z-scale decides
 * what one step of the integer is worth, so it trades the range against the
 * resolution. The z-scale of a file that holds heights between -80 and +80
 * micrometres in int16_t values is about 2.4e-9 metres. A file of a
 * floating-point type usually holds metres directly, with the z-scale 1, but
 * any z-scale is valid.
 *
 * The library offers both views, and a program uses the highest one that
 * suffices. sdfio_read_data() and sdfio_write_data() transfer the coded values
 * as the file has them, row by row. sdfio_decode_heights() and
 * sdfio_encode_heights() convert rows between coded values and heights in
 * metres and need only a header. sdfio_read_heights() and
 * sdfio_write_heights() transfer the whole matrix as heights in one call.
 * Encoding rounds an integer to the nearest value. A height that does not fit
 * the data type with the z-scale is an error (SDFIO_ERR_RANGE), and so is a
 * height that would be written as the bad value (SDFIO_ERR_BAD_VALUE), because
 * a reader would take it for a point that is not measured.
 * sdfio_suggest_z_scale_for_range() and sdfio_suggest_z_scale() find the
 * smallest z-scale that fits the heights, and sdfio_write_heights() uses it
 * when the header has SDFIO_AUTO_Z_SCALE.
 *
 * A point that is not measured has the bad value of the data type as a coded
 * value. A height in metres has no such value, so it is NaN. The functions that read
 * or convert heights deliver NaN for a point that is not measured, and the
 * functions that write or convert heights take NaN and infinity as such a
 * point. This is also the convention of ISO 25178-72 for the binary float
 * formats. A program whose data uses a marker such as -9999 replaces it with
 * NaN before writing, and NaN with the marker after reading.
 *
 * @section format_trailer Trailer
 * The trailer holds historical information about the measurement, such as the
 * name of the operator, the measurement conditions or the processing that was
 * applied. It is optional, has a variable length, and extends to the end of the
 * file. ISO-1.0 does not define its content, and the trailer of an ISO-2.0 file
 * has the form of the header, a line `Name = Value` for each field. The
 * standard asks for text in 7-bit ASCII, which the library does not check: it
 * reads and writes the bytes as they are. The lines of a trailer end with a
 * carriage return and a line feed in the file. The library returns every line
 * end as a line feed, and writes every line end of a trailer as a carriage
 * return and a line feed. sdfio_trailer_find_field() and
 * sdfio_trailer_next_field() find the fields in the text that
 * sdfio_read_trailer() returns, and give the name and the value as offsets into
 * that text.
 *
 * @section format_binary Binary Format
 * The records follow each other without a separator. Numbers are stored with
 * the least significant byte first, and the library converts the byte order. A
 * manufacturer-specific identifier of fewer than 10 characters is filled with
 * spaces.
 *
 * @section format_ascii ASCII Format
 * The file consists of 7-bit ASCII characters in lines that end with a carriage
 * return and a line feed. The first line is the magic, followed by a line
 * `Name = value` for each field of the header, whose names do not depend on the
 * case. Each record ends with a line that consists of the character `*`, and
 * the last such line ends the file. The values of the data area are separated
 * by white space of any length. A `float` is written with 7 significant digits
 * and a two-digit exponent, as in `1.500000e+00`, a `double` with 15 digits and
 * a three-digit exponent, as in `1.50000000000000e+000`, and an integer in
 * decimal digits.
 *
 * @code
 * aISO-2.0
 * ManufacID = libsdfio
 * CreateDate = 150620261030
 * ModDate = 150620261045
 * NumPoints = 3
 * NumProfiles = 2
 * Xscale = 2.50000000000000e-006
 * Yscale = 2.50000000000000e-006
 * Zscale = 1.00000000000000e+000
 * Zresolution = 5.00000000000000e-010
 * Compression = 0
 * DataType = 3
 * CheckType = 0
 * *
 * -1.250000e-06 3.400000e-07 8.100000e-07
 * 2.200000e-07 BAD 1.050000e-06
 * *
 * OperatorName = Jane Doe
 * *
 * @endcode
 */
